::STAT_U03_L05_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which sequence shows exponential growth?
{
~A. 2, 4, 6, 8#This adds 2 each time, so it is linear.
~B. 16, 14, 12, 10#This subtracts 2 each time.
~C. 2, 3, 5, 8#This does not use a constant multiplier.
=D. 2, 4, 8, 16#Each value is multiplied by 2.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which sequence shows exponential decay?
{
~A. 100, 90, 80, 70#This subtracts 10 each time, so it is linear.
~B. 10, 20, 30, 40#This adds 10 each time.
~C. 5, 7, 10, 14#This does not use one constant multiplier.
=D. 100, 50, 25, 12.5#Each value is multiplied by 0.5.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 75(1.2)ˣ, what is the starting value?
{
~A. 1.2#This is the growth factor.
~B. 90#This is the value after one step.
~C. x#x is the input variable.
=D. 75#The starting value is the number multiplied by the exponential factor.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 75(1.2)ˣ, what is the growth factor?
{
=A. 1.2#The factor inside parentheses is multiplied each step.
~B. 75#This is the starting value.
~C. 20#This confuses 20 percent with the factor.
~D. x#x is the input variable.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
A growth factor of 1.2 means what percent increase each step?
{
=A. 20 percent increase#1.2 means 100 percent plus 20 percent.
~B. 1.2 percent increase#The factor 1.2 equals a 20 percent increase, not 1.2 percent.
~C. 80 percent decrease#A factor greater than 1 indicates growth.
~D. 120 percent decrease#A decrease cannot be represented by a factor greater than 1.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
A decay factor of 0.80 means what percent decrease each step?
{
~A. 80 percent decrease#Keeping 80 percent means losing 20 percent.
=B. 20 percent decrease#1 - 0.80 = 0.20, so the decrease is 20 percent.
~C. 8 percent decrease#0.80 is not the same as 8 percent.
~D. 180 percent increase#A factor less than 1 shows decay.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Use y = 10(3)ˣ to predict y when x = 2.
{
~A. 30#This uses x = 1, not x = 2.
~B. 60#This multiplies by 3 and by 2 instead of using 3².
~C. 100#This does not follow the model.
=D. 90#10(3)² = 10(9) = 90.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Use y = 50(0.5)ˣ to predict y when x = 2.
{
~A. 50#This is the starting value at x = 0.
=B. 12.5#50(0.5)² = 50(0.25) = 12.5.
~C. 25#This is the value after one step.
~D. 100#This increases instead of decays.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which model shows exponential growth?
{
=A. y = 30(1.4)ˣ#The factor 1.4 is greater than 1.
~B. y = 30(0.7)ˣ#This shows decay because the factor is less than 1.
~C. y = 30 + 4x#This is linear, not exponential.
~D. y = 4x + 30#This is linear, not exponential.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which model shows exponential decay?
{
~A. y = 90(1.1)ˣ#This shows growth.
~B. y = 90 + 6x#This is linear.
~C. y = 6x + 90#This is linear.
=D. y = 90(0.6)ˣ#The factor 0.6 is between 0 and 1.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
What makes a data pattern exponential rather than linear?
{
~A. It uses two variables#Linear and exponential models both can use two variables.
~B. It has a table#Tables can represent many types of patterns.
~C. It changes by a constant difference#That describes a linear pattern.
=D. It changes by a constant multiplier#Exponential patterns use repeated multiplication.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
What makes a data pattern linear rather than exponential?
{
=A. It changes by a constant difference#Linear patterns add or subtract the same amount each step.
~B. It changes by a constant multiplier#That describes exponential change.
~C. It curves upward#A curve often suggests non-linear growth.
~D. It has a starting value#Both model types can have starting values.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 60(1.15)ˣ, what does 1.15 represent?
{
~A. Add 1.15 each step#Exponential models multiply by the factor.
~B. Start at 1.15#The starting value is 60.
=C. Multiply by 1.15 each step#The factor is the repeated multiplier.
~D. Decrease by 15 percent#A factor greater than 1 indicates increase.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
In y = 60(0.85)ˣ, what does 0.85 represent?
{
~A. Increase by 85 percent#A factor less than 1 shows decay.
=B. Keep 85 percent each step#Keeping 85 percent means decreasing by 15 percent.
~C. Start at 0.85#The starting value is 60.
~D. Add 0.85 each step#Exponential models multiply by the factor.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which prediction is most risky?
{
~A. Predicting one step beyond the observed data#This may still be reasonable if context supports it.
~B. Predicting within the observed data range#This is usually less risky.
=C. Predicting far outside the observed data range#Exponential models can grow or shrink quickly, so far extrapolation is risky.
~D. Predicting at an observed x-value#This is not risky compared with extrapolation.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Why can exponential predictions become unrealistic?
{
=A. Repeated multiplication can grow or shrink very quickly#Fast change can lead to unrealistic long-range predictions.
~B. Exponential models cannot use numbers#They can use numbers.
~C. They always become linear#Exponential models do not become linear by definition.
~D. They prove causation#Models do not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which context often fits exponential growth?
{
~A. A taxi fare increasing by 3 dollars per mile#That is constant addition, so it is linear.
=B. A population doubling over equal time intervals#Doubling is repeated multiplication by 2.
~C. A score increasing by exactly 2 points each week#That is linear.
~D. A balance decreasing by exactly 5 dollars each day#That is linear decrease.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which context often fits exponential decay?
{
~A. A bill increasing by 10 dollars each month#That is linear growth.
~B. Distance increasing by 2 miles every hour#That is linear growth.
=C. Medicine amount decreasing by the same percent each hour#Same percent decrease is exponential decay.
~D. Points increasing by 1 every round#That is linear growth.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Use y = 25(2)ˣ to predict y when x = 3.
{
~A. 75#This multiplies 25 by 3, not by 2³.
=B. 200#25(2)³ = 25(8) = 200.
~C. 100#This uses 2², not 2³.
~D. 250#This does not follow the model.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Use y = 80(0.5)ˣ to predict y when x = 3.
{
=A. 10#80(0.5)³ = 80(0.125) = 10.
~B. 40#This is the value after one step.
~C. 20#This is the value after two steps.
~D. 160#This increases instead of decays.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which phrase best describes exponential association?
{
=A. A curved relationship based on repeated multiplication#Exponential association changes by factors.
~B. A straight relationship based on repeated addition#That describes linear association.
~C. A relationship between two categories only#That describes categorical association, not exponential numerical modeling.
~D. A relationship that always proves causation#No model alone proves causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
The values 7, 14, 28, 56 have what growth factor?
{
~A. 7#The first value is 7, but the repeated multiplier is 2.
~B. 14#This is the second value, not the growth factor.
=C. 2#Each value is multiplied by 2.
~D. 49#This is not the repeated multiplier.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
The values 81, 27, 9, 3 have what decay factor?
{
~A. 3#The values are divided by 3, which means multiplied by 1/3.
~B. 9#This is one of the values, not the factor.
=C. 1/3#Each value is multiplied by 1/3.
~D. 27#This is one of the values, not the factor.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which is the best interpretation of y = 500(0.9)ˣ
{
~A. Start at 0.9 and add 500 each step#This reverses the model parts and uses addition.
=B. Start at 500 and keep 90 percent each step#The model starts at 500 and decays by 10 percent each step.
~C. Start at 500 and increase by 90 percent each step#A factor of 0.9 means decay, not growth.
~D. Start at 500 and subtract 0.9 each step#Exponential models multiply, not subtract.
}

::STAT_U03_L05_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L05_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.6
Question:
Which is the best interpretation of y = 500(1.08)ˣ
{
~A. Start at 1.08 and add 500 each step#This reverses the model parts and uses addition.
~B. Start at 500 and decrease by 8 percent each step#A factor greater than 1 shows growth.
=C. Start at 500 and increase by 8 percent each step#A factor of 1.08 means 8 percent growth each step.
~D. Start at 500 and increase by 108 percent each step#A factor of 1.08 means 108 percent of the previous value, or 8 percent increase.
}
